# The problem

Writing a single PRD means scattering it across five tools: Google Docs for prose,
Sheets for tables, Miro for diagrams, Notion as the hub, Jira for the tasks it
spawns. 
The cost is not the writing. It is everything after.

@note this is the whole bet

@note **Context is lost on the seams.** The diagram explaining the paragraph lives in
  a different tool from the paragraph.
- **Versions drift silently.** The board gets updated, the doc that embeds it does
  not, and nothing warns anyone.
- **Reconstruction is expensive.** Someone joining in month three cannot work out
  where anything lives, so they ask — and the cost lands on whoever wrote it.
- **There is no diff.** *"What changed in this spec since last sprint?"* has no
  cheap answer.

@divider

@heading What makes this one different

Solving fragmentation alone gets you another all-in-one tool, and there are many.
What makes Jotstak worth choosing is what comes out the other end.

@quote None of them treat the document as an artifact worth caring about.

@note notebook is the differentiator, not the pitch

The bet: documents people enjoy re-reading get re-read, and re-read documents keep
teams aligned. Aesthetic is a retention feature, not decoration.

@decision title="Fragmentation leads, the notebook differentiates" status=accepted date=2026-09-18
  context: Two framings were live and contradicted each other — tool fragmentation, and documents treated as freight nobody reopens.
  choice: Lead with fragmentation as the pain; keep the notebook rendering as the reason to pick this over another all-in-one.
  consequences: Sets the landing headline, the README opening, and what the survey is actually testing.

@note settled 18 Sept
somebody \n shout 
@sticky 
  @note


The deciding argument was falsifiability. *"Would you like nicer documents?"* gets
a yes from everyone and tells you nothing. *"How many tools did your last PRD
touch?"* can come back and say the premise is wrong.

## The risk, held open

This still rests on a single-user hypothesis — the author's own pain. That is a
legitimate origin, and many tools started there, but it stays named rather than
hidden until the survey returns data.

